When Priya Nair began teaching at a primary school in Leeds, she assumed the hardest part of the job would be keeping children busy. It turned out to be the opposite. Given ten spare minutes, her pupils would form a queue at her desk, all with the same question: what shall I do now?

{{41}}

Their days, she realised, had been organised so thoroughly that nobody had ever left them alone with an empty afternoon. Boredom, that most ordinary of childhood experiences, had been quietly engineered out of their lives.

{{42}}

Parents were sceptical, and a few wrote to the head to say so. The first weeks were, by Nair's own admission, painful. The children sighed, stared out of the window and complained loudly that this was the worst lesson they had ever had.

{{43}}

By December the classroom was full of invented games, and the walls were covered with maps, comics and board designs that no one had asked for.

{{44}}

Nair's pupils would agree on that point. “The first ten minutes are awful,” says one eleven-year-old, “and then your head just starts.”

{{45}}

For Nair, the whole value of the hour lies in the absence of a plan. The moment an adult steps in with a suggestion, she argues, the children stop looking for their own, and the hour becomes one more activity on the timetable.

{{46}}
